(Regression test for casts in arithmetic. Three bugs are exercised.
 1. Cast-to-float in arithmetic used to take the integer path and operate
    on the float's bit pattern, so {s as a float} add 1 printed 3.5 and
    {s as a float} multiply 2 printed -0.0. Fixed by teaching the float
    detector about Expr::Cast.
 2. Cast precedence used to bind looser than arithmetic, so `s as a number
    add 1` was a parse error and `100 add s as a number` cast the whole
    additive expr. Now a cast binds tight and applies to the expression
    immediately to its left; brace to cast a whole expression.
 3. Bare text in arithmetic used to compile to pointer arithmetic and print
    garbage; it now errors at analysis time. Casts do atoi/atof and pass.
 A variable's declared type is now fixed for good (plan 294): reassigning
 `dyn` to a number is a compile error, so arithmetic on it goes through an
 explicit cast at the use site instead of relying on dynamic relabeling.)

a text called s is "42".
a text called sf is "3.5".
a number called tight1 is s as a number add 1.
a number called tight2 is 100 add s as a number.
a number called rounded is {3.7 add 0.5} as a number.
a number called ic is {s as a number} add 1.
a float called fc1 is {sf as a float} add 1.
a float called fc2 is {sf as a float} multiply 2.
a float called fc3 is {sf as a float} subtract 0.5.
a text called dyn is "5".
a number called dynr is dyn as a number add 1.
Print tight1.
Print tight2.
Print rounded.
Print ic.
Print fc1.
Print fc2.
Print fc3.
Print dynr.